5:## 1. The argument
6:
7:For the qualified profile `sherlock-helper-microstep/1` with committed bounds, let `R(P, s, s')` be the owner-step relation, `Enc` the relation from semantic state to `sherlock-c6-phys/1` carriers satisfying `StepInv`, and `C` the keyed leaf relation with statement `sherlock-leaf-stmt/1`. The obligations, each with its evidence class:
8:
9:| # | Obligation (universal unless stated) | Finite release evidence required by this change | What discharges the universal claim | What cannot |
10:|---|---|---|---|---|
11:| O1 | Given a committed profile, layout and bounds, canonical admission and genesis establish `StepInv` and `Enc` for the initial carriers bound to `prog_d` and `init_d` | initialization cases (task 4.9): canonical genesis admitted and bound; non-canonical, inconsistent `init_d`/`prog_d`, and forged initial carriers refused at the leaf/node boundary | a theorem over the codec and genesis constraints (SAS-009) | host `checkStepInv` |
12:| O2 | Soundness per rule, given `StepInv(pre)` and a valid image/configuration: every satisfying witness of `C` with `sel[r]=1` decodes to a valid `R` step by rule `r`, preserves `StepInv`, and exposes exactly the statement | hostile raw witnesses by dispatch class with a valid baseline and weakened-constraint controls (SAS-004, SAS-013), refusal observed at the constraint boundary | a theorem, or an exhaustive checked argument over the stated domain (SAS-009) | finite tests alone; `ProofPreimage::check` |
13:| O3 | Completeness per rule: every valid bounded step has a satisfying witness | differential run on every step of P1, P2 and (after freeze) P3 through the witness stream (SVM-007) plus real proofs | a theorem | model agreement |
14:| O4 | Composition: the one-hot leaf checks every fragment premise on the shared carriers (no disconnected copies), with `StepInv(pre)` initialization and preservation | SAS-010 directed cases per admitted rule; shared-carrier premise checks (task 4.6) | a theorem | per-rule circuit passes; "one key" by itself |
15:| O5 | Recursion: node links `post_d = next pre_d`, `h` arithmetic bounded, authenticated genesis, authorized keys | SRC-004/005 raw witnesses at the node boundary and through the final pairing; flipped-proof refusal; nonmember-key refusal under F | a proof or recorded security argument for the IVC relation and the genesis construction | `ToyLeaf` tests (`sherlock_ivc.rs:68-72`) |
16:| O6 | Terminal closure: decider pins keys, pairs, derives outcome/result | SRC-006 mutations with fresh witnesses; 70-lane record labelled existing-proof binding | a proof or recorded security argument for the decider relation and the pairing | outer-only success |
17:| O7 | Target (scope-limited to the observed chain instance and operation): result bound to chain instance, call, key, effects; unique consumption | A6-shape local acceptance, A8p/A9p/A10p manifest-bound predicates on Preview (SAS-007, SMN-004) | a scope-limited target observation; no universal claim exists for O7 | any `ContractCall` at the address; stagenet evidence for a mainnet claim |
18:
19:A formal proof names its definitions and premises (SAS-009). Finite tests give finite correspondence on their declared domain. O2 and O3 are universally quantified; no finite hostile or differential suite closes them. The release gates for this change are therefore *finite* gates with the premise `StepInv(pre)` stated: hostile classes covered, directed cases run, differential agreement on P1-P3. The universal obligations stay open and labelled until a theorem artifact exists (astra-2). No class substitutes for another (UISEC-012).
