1:# order: measurement plan, budgets, memory safety and the new task order
2:
3:Agent `order`, 2026-11-08. Scope per BRIEF.md: the C0 phase profile, mandatory throughput budgets and gates, the re-ordered tasks.md, memory safety. Read-only; nothing was built or proved. Every number marked ESTIMATE has its arithmetic beside it.
4:
5:## 1. Findings, ranked
6:
7:1. **The host had no memory guard while I read.** `cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast` (run `20261108T043038Z-m2-2.1-test-workspace`, lease `~/.cache/doctorwatson/heavy.lock.lease`) ran a debug `mori_backend` test binary at VmRSS 112.4 GB, VmHWM 117.1 GB, VmSwap 21.5 GB, 41 threads, on the 120 GB host (`/proc/2334253/status`, 04:4xZ). The heavy wrapper enforces an RSS cap only when a budget cap exists (`models/doctorwatson/factory/heavy.py:416-419`, `:190-195`); this run had `--max-wall` only, so `cap_rss` was None. Preflight checks disk, load and the lock, never `MemAvailable` or swap (`preflight.py:53-66`). zram swap is 120 GB (`/proc/swaps`), so a runaway job compresses into RAM instead of failing. The job later ended and memory returned to 12 GB used. Section 5 proposes the fix. It must land before any further heavy run.
8:2. **C0 is implemented up to the run; the run never happened, and the harness cannot profile the node chain yet.** Tasks 1-4.3 and 3.1-3.4 of `openspec/changes/recursion-phase-profile/tasks.md` are ticked; 5.1, 5.2, 6.x, 7.1 are open. Only `profile-a2` exists (`tools/profile/src/bin/profile-a2.rs`); `planned_commands` names `profile-recursion` for A3/A4/B3 and `--run` refuses when the file is missing (`scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py:604,657-659`). No workload covers leaf, node or decider of the GP chain. The profile workspace pins `midnight-zk-stdlib =2.3.5` (`tools/profile/Cargo.toml`), while the main workspace is at 2.4.0 / circuits 7.3.0 after 2.1 (`Cargo.toml:43-45`); `midnight-proofs` stays 0.8.3 in both, so the vendored patch stays valid, but `tools/profile/Cargo.lock` must be regenerated after 2.1 lands.
9:3. **The node chain is the only thing worth profiling for throughput.** Leaf k13 proves in 2.55 s; node k20 95 s/step, 26.2 GB; decider k20 about 100 s, 16.5 GB (`docs/sherlock/stagenet-gp-recursion.md:184-201`). fact(3) at S=1: 293 x 95 s = 27,835 s = 7.7 h ESTIMATE. The 2 h target needs 293 x t_node + 100 s < 7,200 s, so t_node < 24.2 s at S=1 (ESTIMATE: (7200-100)/293). No single CPU lever reaches a 3.9x cut on the node alone. The target is met only by node count reduction (S>1), or GPU plus k19, or both. The order below reflects that.
10:4. **k=19 is a row problem, not a profile problem.** The node is 531,989 rows, 7,701 over 2^19 = 524,288 (1.47%). Its own source says the shared MSM was meant to keep it at k19 (`crates/mori-recursion/src/ivc.rs:351-352`). The 2.3 node fixes add rows (three 64-bit range checks). The cut must be found with `cost_model` at T1 before keygen 2.4, or the keys are regenerated twice. Expected gain ESTIMATE 1.5-1.9x on node prove and about half the memory: all domain-proportional phases (NTT, quotient, MSM over 2^k, products) halve; synthesis and key load do not. The profile fixes the exact factor (section 2.5).
11:5. **Thread count is an unmeasured confounder.** Every check script forces `RAYON_NUM_THREADS=4` (`scripts/qa/check-a3.sh:6`, `check-a4.sh:6`, `check-b3.sh:7`); the 95 s/step was measured by the `sherlock_gp` test under heavy.lock with an unrecorded thread count. The sidecar records the host, so the C0 run must sweep 4/10/20 threads. If 95 s was a 4-thread number, 20 threads could give up to 2-3x ESTIMATE on the parallel phases (upper bound 20/4 = 5x on perfectly parallel phases; Amdahl with 30% serial gives 1/(0.3+0.7/5) = 2.27x). Cheapest lever on the list; measure it first.
12:6. **S>1 is the biggest lever and it must be decided before the universal leaf is keyed.** S=8 gives ceil(293/8) = 37 nodes: 37 x 95 s + 100 s = 3,615 s = 1.0 h at k20 ESTIMATE; with k19 at 60 s: 37 x 60 + 100 = 2,320 s = 39 min ESTIMATE. The leaf VK is a node constant and the node VK a decider constant (design §5.2(2)), so every leaf change regenerates node and decider keys. Keying the leaf at S=1 in 4.4 and again at S>1 in 4.8 is a second full cascade. Section 4 chooses S at gate G-A from the k19 node time and keys the leaf once; S=1 becomes the run-time case "one enabled slot" (SRC-014 structure present from the first key).
13:7. **Tree aggregation buys wall time only through concurrency, and concurrency is memory-bound.** Linear IVC and a binary tree both prove N-1 nodes; the tree shortens the critical path to log2(N) only if several node provers run at once. Owner decision 2026-11-08: "48 GB per process, 100 GB aggregate". At 26.2 GB a node that admits P=3 concurrent k20 provers (3 x 26.2 = 78.6 GB; P=4 is 104.8 GB, over the cap) and, at k19 (ESTIMATE about 14 GB), P=4 or more until the CPU saturates (4 x 14 = 56 GB). A node that verifies two node proofs instead of node+leaf has more rows than today (the in-circuit verifier cost grows with the child VK's columns, and a k20 node VK is wider than a k13 leaf VK), so the tree node is k20, not k19: ESTIMATE 531,989 + 150k to 250k rows, under 2^20 but over 2^19. A k20 tree node at P=3 and a k19 linear node are therefore the two shapes to compare, not combine. Concurrency gains nothing when one prover already saturates the 20 cores. The deciding numbers are the tree agent's (tree.md §7): f3 = wall of one of three concurrent node proofs at 6 threads / solo wall, and U = average cores of one node at 20 threads; equivalently E = U/20 (section 2.5). The research cluster s7 reached the same reading: "every child-carried accumulator must also be consumed; current leaf aggregator is not a generic recursive tree" and at most 1.20-1.50x in bulk work (`wiki-llm/recursion-perf/clusters/s7-recursion/wave-1/FINDINGS.md:28,37`). Keep the tree as a measured option behind E11 (`workflow.md:115`), not as a build item.
14:8. **GPU: the toolchain is one owner action away; the libraries are the risk.** `nvidia-smi` sees the RTX Spark N1X (driver 615.78.08, CUDA UMD 13.4, compute capability 12.1 per `~/hardware-review/REPORT.txt`); `libcuda.so.1` is installed; `nvcc` is not. The `cuda 13.4.3-1` package exists for aarch64 in `extra` (`pacman -Si cuda`). The CUDA smoke test passed (`~/hardware-review/cuda-smoke.json`). The host is aarch64: sppark "primarily supports x86_64" and its ARM64 note covers only non-GPU parts (https://github.com/supranational/sppark); ICICLE's CUDA backend is not MIT ("distributed under a special license", https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle) and names no architecture; the s3 cluster recorded an ICICLE Montgomery chunking bug and sppark per-call syncs (`wiki-llm/recursion-perf/clusters/s3-commit-msm/wave-1/FINDINGS.md:9,42,52`). NVIDIA forum threads on DGX Spark (GB10, sm_121) report CUDA libraries needing source builds for aarch64 and older nvcc not knowing compute_121 (https://forums.developer.nvidia.com/t/cupqc-sdk-0-4-1-crashes-on-dgx-spark-gb10-sm-121-does-blackwell-support-exist/361506). A GPU MSM changes no proof bytes when its group result is exact (`msm_specific`, `tools/profile/vendor/midnight-proofs-0.8.3/src/poly/kzg/msm.rs:163-191`, is a pure function), so soundness is a differential test, not a protocol change; but it needs a patched midnight-proofs in the production prover, which RPP-007 forbids for the profile workspace and the main workspace today. Gain bound: Amdahl on the measured MSM share m: 1/(1-m) at infinite speedup; m=0.3 gives at most 1.43x, m=0.6 at most 2.5x. The profile decides whether the GPU work is opened at all.
15:9. **The factory can enforce memory with the kernel.** `systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=64M -p MemorySwapMax=0` killed a 300 MB allocation with exit 137 on this host; the user slice delegates `cpu memory pids` (`/sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-1000.slice/cgroup.controllers`). The 200 ms RSS poll in `heavy.py:190-195` can stay as the measurement; the cgroup becomes the guard.
16:10. **The factory's measure-then-cap rule would set the throughput caps at 2x whatever is first measured** (`CONTRACT.md:85`, `budgets.py:71`). For requirement caps (2 h, 48 GB) the `cap` must be seeded in `budgets.json` with `measured` null; `heavy` then enforces the seeded cap and never overwrites it (`heavy.py:448` records a measurement only when `cap is None`).
17:
18:## 1b. Owner requirement 2026-11-08: general-purpose recursion, reasoned about in Lean and Quint
19:
20:Verbatim: "whatever approach you choose have some agents explore it with lean and quint to reason about its design. It has to be general purpose recursion".
21:
22:**Fixed keys.** One key set (leaf over the frozen inventory `R` at the chosen S, node, decider, acceptRun template) serves every committed program and every step count. The linear self-recursive node already has this property: the node key is a constant of itself (`ivc.rs:295-301`), the decider pins `vk_N_repr`, and depth is unbounded. S>1 keeps it as long as S is a keyed constant and the final chunk pads (SRC-014). Option F keeps it because the family table is fixed before P3. Two things in the record violate it and are cut here: per-program or per-depth keys ("fixed layers, separate keys per level", `docs/sherlock/stagenet-gp-recursion.md:168`) and a tree whose shape is keyed by the leaf count. A tree is admissible only as a self-recursive node that accepts node or leaf children under the same key, with a padding rule; that is a design to prove before it is built.
23:
24:**What exists to build on.** Quint: `models/sherlock/quint/universalMachine.qnt`, `kProfileMachine.qnt` (reference machine), `models/recursion/pipeline.qnt` (scheduler, no linkage), `models/recursion/profile.qnt` (C0 accounting, the pattern RPP 1.1 used: predicates plus seeded mutants, `quint test`). Quint 0.32.0 is installed. Lean: `clean/fork/` (Lean `v4.33.1`, `lakefile.lean` library `SherlockSpec`, globs `Clean.Sherlock`, build dir `target/sherlock-lean`) with the statement/proof split `X.lean` + `XAxioms.lean` (for example `StagePackage.lean:68-74`), zero `sorry`. The toolchain was not installed on this aarch64 host; `lake --version` started the elan download, so the first `lake build` is a network action to run once. No tier runs `lake build` today and no predicate row names a Lean file (UIREQ-026 is a Clean correspondence row, `predicates.yaml:634-639`). Task 5.4 of the current tasks.md already asks for a Quint chain-invariant model (`assurance.md:44`); it moves forward and grows.
25:
26:**Formal tasks (gate the implementation).** Both produce artifacts a T0 check runs; both are `[loop]` once the brief is written and `[lead]` for the statement review.
27:
28:- **2.11 Quint model of the aggregation protocol**, `models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt` + `aggregation_test.qnt`: states = multiset of proven claims `(prog_d, init_d, pre_d, post_d, h_pre, steps, acc)`; actions = `leaf(S slots, enabled count)`, `fold(node, leaf)` (linear), `join(node, node)` (tree, guarded by a module constant `TREE`), `pad` (disabled slots and padding children), `decide`, `replay(stmt)`, `schedule` (any interleaving of independent folds). Invariants: linkage (`post_d` of the consumed child equals `pre_d` of the next; heights add); unique genesis (`h_pre = 0` iff `pre_d = init_d`, at most once per chain); bounded monotone height (`h < 2^64`, strictly increasing by `steps in [1,S]`); padding neutrality (a padded slot or padding child changes no digest and adds zero steps); replay (the decider's `stmt` set refuses a second acceptance); schedule independence (every admissible interleaving of `join` yields the same root claim: same `(prog_d, init_d, fin_d, n_steps)`); fixed keys (every proof in every trace is checked under one of three key identities). Mutants: drop the range check, scale the genesis carry wrongly, let a padded slot count a step, accept a replayed stmt, let two roots differ by schedule. Verify: `quint test models/sherlock/quint/aggregation_test.qnt` passes and every mutant is caught; `quint run --invariant` with `TREE=true` and `TREE=false`. -> row F20 (T0). Gates 2.3 (node constraints match the model's `fold`) and 5.7 (tree only if the `TREE=true` invariants hold).
29:- **2.12 Lean composition theorems**, `clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean` + `AggregationAxioms.lean`, in the existing `SherlockSpec` library: define `Step` (the reference machine's step relation as a parameter), `Claim`, `Leaf`, `Node`, `Root`; theorems: (i) soundness of composition: a valid root claim `(prog_d, init_d, fin_d, n)` implies a sequence of `n` valid machine steps from the state behind `init_d` to the state behind `fin_d` for the program behind `prog_d`, for every program (quantified over `Program`, not over P1-P3), under the premises that each leaf relation is sound for its S slots and the digest is injective on the carrier domain; (ii) associativity: `join (join a b) c = join a (join b c)` on claims, so any fold schedule gives the same root; (iii) padding neutrality: `join a pad = a` and a leaf with `k` enabled slots followed by `S-k` disabled slots proves exactly `k` steps; (iv) height arithmetic: heights add without overflow under the range checks. The in-circuit verifier, the accumulator and the pairing are premises (axiom file), as `assurance.md:26` classifies them; the theorems are about composition, not cryptography. Verify: `lake build` in `clean/fork` with the new module, no `sorry`, `#print axioms` lists only the declared premises. -> row F21 (T0 after the toolchain is present; the check runs `lake build` with the shared build dir and skips with exit 2 while `~/.elan` lacks the toolchain, so the first run is an owner-visible install). Gates 4.2 (the leaf's slot semantics are the theorem's `Leaf`) and 5.7.
30:
31:Rows: **F20 aggregation-quint** (T0, `check: quint test models/sherlock/quint/aggregation_test.qnt --main aggregation_test` plus the mutant script, `source: openspec/.../design.md#3.1`), **F21 aggregation-lean** (T0, `check: scripts/qa/check-lean-aggregation.sh`, which runs `lake build SherlockSpec` in `clean/fork` and greps `#print axioms` output against the allowed premise list). Requirement **SRC-017 (M) general-purpose fixed keys**: the leaf, node and decider keys SHALL be independent of the program, the initial state and the step count; the aggregation protocol SHALL have a Quint model whose invariants (linkage, padding neutrality, replay, schedule independence, fixed keys) pass with seeded mutants caught, and Lean theorems (composition soundness for every program, associativity, padding neutrality, height arithmetic) that build without `sorry`, before the node constraints (2.3), the leaf slots (4.2) or any tree node (5.7) are implemented. Tasks 2.3, 4.2 and 5.7 list F20 and F21 as `depends`.
32:
33:## 2. C0 phase profile
34:
35:### 2.1 What exists and what is missing
36:
37:Exists (ticked with observed outputs): Quint accounting model; vendored midnight-proofs 0.8.3 with the default-off `recursion-phase-profile` feature and RAII spans at the RPP-002 boundaries; harness crate with key-load/setup_pk/prove/verify timers, 10 ms RSS sampler, process CPU and `/proc/self/io` counters; `profile-recursion.py prepare/calibrate/one/reduce/decide`; 61 Python tests, 20 Rust integration tests; `profile-a2` binary; T0 isolation check.
38:
39:Missing: (a) a binary for the recursion chain (leaf, node, decider); (b) the `GP` workload in `BUDGETS` and `planned_commands` (`profile-recursion.py:23-24,595-624`); (c) re-pin of `tools/profile/Cargo.toml` to zk-stdlib 2.4.0 / circuits 7.3.0 and a fresh `tools/profile/Cargo.lock` after task 2.1; (d) the paired overhead qualification (5.2); (e) every run (5.1, 6.1-6.3, 7.1); (f) the fixed resident_pk path: `one --workload A2` refuses resident_pk because the public child APIs rebuild the PK (`:602-603`), whereas the node chain holds `node_pk` for the whole fold (`crates/mori-recursion/tests/sherlock_gp.rs:230,267`), so the GP workload supports both cache modes naturally.
40:
41:### 2.2 The command
42:
43:Code first (task 2.7 below): `tools/profile/src/bin/profile-gp.rs` reproduces `gp_fold_and_key` (`sherlock_gp.rs:163-357`) through the public APIs `SherlockIvcRelation::new`, `cost_model`, `setup_vk`, `setup_pk`, `prove`, `verify`, `RunDeciderRelation`, with caller spans around key load, `setup_pk`, each node `prove`, each `verify`, decider keygen, decider prove, ledger `VerifierKey::verify`; stand-in leaves via `MORIARTY_GP_STANDIN_STEPS` or real leaves via `MORIARTY_GP_LEAF_DIR`; `--threads` recorded in the sidecar host block. Then:
44:
45:```sh
46:python3 -B scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py prepare --check-only
47:# overhead qualification, cheap (A2 is 11 s measured, cap 23 s):
48:python3 -B scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py calibrate --workload A2 --pairs 5 --run-id c0-a2 --run
49:# the chain, both variants, resident PK, 3 stand-in leaves, 20 threads:
50:python3 -B scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py one --workload GP --cache-mode resident_pk --run-id c0-gp-t20 --variant both --run
51:# which expands to (per variant):
52:scripts/doctorwatson heavy --id C0-c0-gp-t20-GP-build-on --max-wall 3600 -- env RUSTC_WRAPPER= CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 CARGO_TARGET_DIR=tools/profile/target/on cargo build --offline --locked --release --manifest-path tools/profile/Cargo.toml --bin profile-gp --features recursion-phase-profile
53:scripts/doctorwatson heavy --id C0-c0-gp-t20-GP-resident_pk-0-on --budget c0-gp-chain -- env MORIARTY_PROFILE_EVENTS=qa/sherlock/profiles/c0-gp-t20/GP-resident_pk-0-on.events.json MORIARTY_GP_STANDIN_STEPS=3 RAYON_NUM_THREADS=20 tools/profile/target/on/release/profile-gp --workload GP --cache-mode resident_pk --sidecar qa/sherlock/profiles/c0-gp-t20/GP-resident_pk-0-on.json
54:# repeat with --run-id c0-gp-t4 (RAYON_NUM_THREADS=4) and c0-gp-t10
55:python3 -B scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py reduce --run c0-gp-t20
56:python3 -B scripts/qa/profile-recursion.py decide --run c0-gp-t20
57:```
58:
59:`reduce` enforces the 5% coverage rule and the exclusive partition (`profile-recursion.py:192-230`); `decide` writes one metric or reason per row C1-C10 (`:507-568`). Add C11-C14 rows for the four levers (section 2.5) in the same function.
60:
61:### 2.3 Time and memory (ESTIMATE)
62:
63:Per GP run with 3 stand-in leaves, from the recorded numbers: SRS load + leaf keygen about 30 s; node keygen 50 s; 3 node steps 3 x 95 = 285 s; node verifies about 3 x 2 s; decider keygen 53 s; decider prove 100 s; verifies about 5 s. Total about 525 s = 8.8 min. On/off pair 17.5 min. Three thread settings x one pair = 53 min. Builds: two release variants of the profile workspace, cold, ESTIMATE 10-20 min each (the 2.1 debug build of three crates took 46 s incremental, `runs.jsonl`; a cold release build of the midnight stack is an order of magnitude more). A2 calibration: 5 pairs x (11 s + 11 s) + 2 builds, under 10 min after the builds. Whole C0: ESTIMATE 2-3 h wall, unattended under caps.
64:
65:Memory: the node peak 26.2 GB and decider 16.5 GB are sequential in one process, so peak about 27 GB plus the sampler's bounded buffers (65,536 slots, negligible). Seed `c0-gp-chain` cap at 48 GB / 1,800 s. The profile feature adds no per-cell data (design §Structure).
66:
67:### 2.4 Can it run on the current chain before the universal leaf exists, and what does it say
68:
69:Yes. Two chains exist today: the GP chain with stand-in leaves (`standin.rs`, k13-class) or the real C6 digest leaf from the M2 measurement, and the retired A2-A4 two-child chain (`check-a3.sh`, `check-a4.sh`, stagenet keys). The node, decider and A3 parent are all ZkStdLib verifier relations with the BLS and Poseidon chips at k20 (`bls_arch`, `crates/mori-recursion/src/lib.rs:23-30`), so phase shares transfer between them approximately; but the levers are about the node, and the node exists, so profile the node. The stand-in leaf has the same statement shape as the future universal leaf (seven lanes), so the node's phase profile is the production node's profile up to the leaf VK's column count. What it does not tell us: the universal leaf's own cost at S=1 or S>1 (M4), and the real `entry 7;` chain wall (5.3 repeats the profile there; that is the second, confirming run).
70:
71:### 2.5 Profile outputs that decide each lever
72:
73:All from one named node proof (resident_pk, 20 threads) unless stated; `decide` writes them as rows C11-C14 with the span ids credited.
74:
75:| Lever | Metric from the sidecar | Keep if | Kill or defer if |
76:|---|---|---|---|
77:| Threads (lever 0) | `node_step_wall` at 4, 10, 20 threads; `cpu_core_seconds / wall` | wall(20) <= 0.7 x wall(4): set `RAYON_NUM_THREADS=20` in the chain scripts | wall(20) > 0.9 x wall(4): the prover is serial-bound; record where (phase with lowest CPU/wall) |
78:| k=19 | domain-proportional share d = (quotient + commitment_msm + multiopen + lookup + permutation products) / total; predicted factor 1/(1-d/2); `cost_model` rows after the 2.3 fixes | `cost_model` k == 19 with the fixes in, and measured post-cut `node_step_wall` <= 0.65 x k20 baseline, peak RSS <= 16 GB | rows cannot get under 2^19 - blinding without a chip change: record, keep k20, S carries the target |
79:| S>1 | arithmetic only: `n_steps/S x t_node(k19) + n_steps/S x t_leaf(S) + t_decider`; leaf memory from M4 at the chosen S | S = smallest power of two with 320/S x t_node + 320/S x t_leaf(S) + t_decider <= 0.8 x 7,200 s and leaf k(S) <= 17 + log2(S) and leaf peak RSS <= 16 GB | leaf at S does not fit: step down one power of two; at S=1 the throughput row fails and GPU becomes mandatory |
80:| GPU MSM | m = (`commitment_msm` + multiopen MSM spans) / total (C3 metric plus C9 shared spans); Amdahl bound 1/(1-m) | m >= 0.45 after k19 (bound >= 1.8x) and the aarch64 sm_121 build spike passes the differential test | m < 0.3 (bound < 1.43x) or the spike fails: record, close |
81:| Tree / concurrency (criteria from tree.md §7) | T0 row probe: rows and k of the {self, self, leaf} slot node (`tree-node-rows`, modeling only); T1(a) U = average cores of one node at 20 threads; T1(b) f3 = wall(one of three concurrent, 6 threads) / wall(solo) and the aggregate peak (`node-concurrent-3`) | all of: slot node <= 0.95 x 2^20 rows; f3 <= 1.4 (equivalently U <= 6) and aggregate peak <= 100 GB; projected ceil(L/3) x t x f3 <= 0.5 x L x t | any of: k=21 at T0; f3 >= 2.5; linear at the chosen S already meets 2 h with margin (the tree's marginal gain is then ESTIMATE 2.5-3x for 1-2 weeks of work plus E11) |
82:
83:## 3. Budgets and gates
84:
85:### 3.1 Requirement edits
86:
87:Replace SRC-011 (O) with:
88:
89:> **SRC-011 (M)** A committed program of at most 320 owner steps SHALL prove its leaves, fold them through the node chain or tree, prove the run decider, pass `verify_chain` and the local proof-verifying `validate_call`, in under 2 h wall, under 48 GB peak RSS per process and under 100 GB aggregate peak RSS across the concurrently running heavy processes of the run (owner decision 2026-11-08: "48 GB per process, 100 GB aggregate"), on the 20-core 120 GB host, with `RAYON_NUM_THREADS`, the concurrency P and both peaks recorded in the receipt; as one `heavy` run or a declared concurrent set of runs whose wall is the makespan. The C0 phase profile (RPP) SHALL be committed before any S, concurrency or GPU adoption, and each adoption SHALL cite the C0 metric that justified it. A shortfall is a failing row; it never passes by being recorded. Evidence: `budgets.json` rows `chain-entry7`, `chain-fact3`, `chain-list123`; `qa/sherlock/decisions/speed-levers.json`.
