128:### F7. Why it was cut, and which reasons still hold (SOURCE)
129:
130:`stagenet-gp-recursion.md:146`: "The original proposal (kept for the record) was a binary tree of
131:nodes over per-rule leaves." The reasons visible in `:138,:164-169`, `proposal.md` supersession
132:table, D13 and fable-2's position (`research/sherlock-vm-council-20261107/fable-2/position.md:49`):
133:
134:| Reason then | Holds now? |
135:|---|---|
136:| "per-rule leaf keys cannot feed one node" (`:138`); the tree was bundled with per-rule leaves and cut with them | No. D4 makes the leaf universal; the leaf slot takes one constant key (or the F-table membership check) exactly as `ivc.rs` does. |
137:| Node count is #leaves-1 either way, and proofs run "serial under heavy.lock" (`:167-168`), so a tree bought nothing | Only while the lock is serial. The owner made throughput mandatory; §3.5 already names the second lock class (E11) as the enabling work. |
138:| Linear IVC was the smallest change to `parent.rs`, and M3 proved it (`:190-193`) | Still true. The slot node is a bounded delta on `ivc.rs` (one more self slot, bits, decider pin), not a rewrite. |
139:| recursion-perf s7: "a balanced binary tree adds three internal proofs; the table floor prevents rewarding its smaller advice count" (FINDINGS, four fixed rollup leaves) | Not applicable: that is the 16-tx rollup with four leaves, where arity-4 needs one node. Sherlock has 37-293 leaves in a dependency chain. |
140:| SRC-011 Optional; D13 recorded the tree as a fallback because nothing was measured | Reversed by the owner. Measurement is this plan's first step. |
