research/sherlock-speed-replan-20261108/order.md:7:1. **The host had no memory guard while I read.** `cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast` (run `20261108T043038Z-m2-2.1-test-workspace`, lease `~/.cache/doctorwatson/heavy.lock.lease`) ran a debug `mori_backend` test binary at VmRSS 112.4 GB, VmHWM 117.1 GB, VmSwap 21.5 GB, 41 threads, on the 120 GB host (`/proc/2334253/status`, 04:4xZ). The heavy wrapper enforces an RSS cap only when a budget cap exists (`models/doctorwatson/factory/heavy.py:416-419`, `:190-195`); this run had `--max-wall` only, so `cap_rss` was None. Preflight checks disk, load and the lock, never `MemAvailable` or swap (`preflight.py:53-66`). zram swap is 120 GB (`/proc/swaps`), so a runaway job compresses into RAM instead of failing. The job later ended and memory returned to 12 GB used. Section 5 proposes the fix. It must land before any further heavy run.
research/sherlock-speed-replan-20261108/order.md:9:3. **The node chain is the only thing worth profiling for throughput.** Leaf k13 proves in 2.55 s; node k20 95 s/step, 26.2 GB; decider k20 about 100 s, 16.5 GB (`docs/sherlock/stagenet-gp-recursion.md:184-201`). fact(3) at S=1: 293 x 95 s = 27,835 s = 7.7 h ESTIMATE. The 2 h target needs 293 x t_node + 100 s < 7,200 s, so t_node < 24.2 s at S=1 (ESTIMATE: (7200-100)/293). No single CPU lever reaches a 3.9x cut on the node alone. The target is met only by node count reduction (S>1), or GPU plus k19, or both. The order below reflects that.
research/sherlock-speed-replan-20261108/order.md:12:6. **S>1 is the biggest lever and it must be decided before the universal leaf is keyed.** S=8 gives ceil(293/8) = 37 nodes: 37 x 95 s + 100 s = 3,615 s = 1.0 h at k20 ESTIMATE; with k19 at 60 s: 37 x 60 + 100 = 2,320 s = 39 min ESTIMATE. The leaf VK is a node constant and the node VK a decider constant (design §5.2(2)), so every leaf change regenerates node and decider keys. Keying the leaf at S=1 in 4.4 and again at S>1 in 4.8 is a second full cascade. Section 4 chooses S at gate G-A from the k19 node time and keys the leaf once; S=1 becomes the run-time case "one enabled slot" (SRC-014 structure present from the first key).
